A||||13||JAMIE||V6 compatibility 85 to 88||||1||
Z||000000||JAMIE||10-26-2000||04:21 PM||||Does anyone know whether an original V6 Fiero motor (unknown year) will fit perfectly in a Fiero that has a V6 already - ie, can an 85GT 86GT 87GT etc. motor be dropped straight into an 88 GT - or are there some significant issues to contend with.<P>Also - is there a serial number somewhere on the V6 that can be deciphered to get year of manufacture?||||reg||1
Z||000001||william_thorniii||10-26-2000||04:44 PM||||Jamie,<P>It can be done.  It might not be desireable though.  The most significant difference between the 88 2.8L and the other years is the fact that the 88 is internally balanced with balance shafts.  The pre-88's used a weighted flywheel to counteract the engine's dynamic loading.  So you would definitely need the flywheel from the other 2.8L. <P>Beyond that consideration, there is the additional consideration of what it would do to NVH.  The 88 cradle mounts the engine differently.  I am not sure if the differnt balancing of the engine was what drove the mounting changes or not... Anyone else know?  <P>fiero56 might be able to shed some light on the subject... since he is planning on putting an 86 engine in his formula temorarily in the near future.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000002||Uaana||10-26-2000||08:40 PM||||If the 88 v6 is so different, then why do the rebuild kits on the Fiero store have 85-86 rebuild kits, and 87-88 rebuild kits?<BR>Were there other differences other than the internal balance on the 88?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||10-26-2000||09:59 PM||||Uaana,<P>The differences in the rebuild kits is the oil pan gaskets. The '85 & '86 have different oil pans and different timing case covers. <BR>Jamie,<P>There are also some wiring differences on the bolt ons, just be sure to use the ones off the '88 and you'll be OK. The only differences that I can see is that the '88's front engine mount is different (also uses a different mount to frame) and there is an additional mount on the 'rear' side of the engine (my only be for automatics). I have an '88 engine on the hoist and an '85 block being built and see no casting numbers that identify year. (doesn't there aren't any!)<BR>The only 'balance shafts' that I'm aware of in any Fiero engine is in the '88 2.5 engine oil pump.||||reg||1
Z||000004||sdm1964||10-27-2000||06:28 AM||||The cars vin number is stamped into the engine block right above the starter. When decoded it will tell you what year and everything about the vehicle it came out of.||||reg||7
Z||000005||JAMIE||10-27-2000||08:03 AM||||Thanks to all of you.I did some digging and found what I think is the correct info.(I have Chilton,Haynes,Factory Service Mamual and P22 - it is amazing that none of these were clear on this issue and often appeared to conflict with each other).<P>As best as I can gather, location of stamp is(when at rear of car facing the engine) on LHS, lower part of block near crank case, and toward the front of the block.<P>The key is in the last 3 letters on the stamp - for example, 1988 is CFC or CFD, 1987  is SCN or SCP , 1986 is DAF or DAH or DBN, 1985 is CBX or CBY.<P>Other tell tale hints are whether "FIERO" on plenum is sticker(85-87) or engraved (88) - although I am not sure about this one  and  the lettering on the oil fill cap varies (Older motors have cap with the words "OIL FILL ONLY" at top of cap in upper case letters and newer ones have "5w30" in the very centre - again not positive when lettering changed  -  maybe someone on the forum knows.<P>If any of above incorrect please post correction.<P>Again, thanks to all who responded.<P>Jamie<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||10-27-2000||11:05 AM||||Jamie,<P>The '87 & '88 upper plenums are both engraved.<BR>Oil fill caps '85 thru '88 (at least ones here) are all the same.<BR>nothing below right cylinder head on flats on the '85. Can't tell on the '88 (not torn down).<BR>Possible Vin # above starter on the '88, can't verify - no car here. May be able to chase it down, I'll work on that later. <BR>All the raised numbers appear to castings only. The cast #'s below #2 cylinder are in the same general location, however the GM logo's are in different locations. The '88 has the logo above cast # and the '85 is below. I'm unsure if this will be consistant between years or not. I'll try to do more research for you later on.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||10-27-2000||12:03 PM||||I posted the same question just a month or so ago. I think it was FieroDave who came through with a complete list of everything needed to do the change. I will search for the thread, and post the link here.||||reg||1
